            </div>
            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="PRESENCE_BASIS" id="PRESENCE_BASIS-window" value="window" <?= $settings['PRESENCE_BASIS'] === 'window' ? 'checked' : '' ?>>
                <label class="form-check-label" for="PRESENCE_BASIS-window">Only the time they are known to have spent at the place</label>
            </div>
            <div class="form-text">
                Applies to every group whose audience is <em>people connected to its places</em> — a group shown to
                everyone has no places to measure time spent at, so it always uses the whole lifespan.
                <br>
                <strong>Whole lifespan</strong> shows an event to anyone connected to a group's places who was alive
                when it happened, even if they had moved away by then, or only arrived afterwards.
                <br>
                <strong>Time spent at the place</strong> is narrower: it looks at the person's own dated facts there
                (for example a birth, a census, or a residence — see <em>Facts that count</em>, below) and only shows
                events from between the earliest and latest of those.
                A person connected to a group only by an undated fact, or only by their burial there, gets
                <strong>no events at all</strong> this way, rather than guessing at their whole lifespan — someone
                whose body was brought to a place for burial did not necessarily ever live there.
                This needs a reasonably detailed tree: a bare birth and death, with nothing else recorded at the
                place, gives only the single year of the birth to work with.
            </div>
        </div>
